ProductRepository will communicate with SQLServer
It will execute queries using dapper.
Finally, return product data to customer.

So, something like
ProductRepository --Queries-->Dapper--->Execute--->SQLServer
SQLserver-->Dapper--->ProductRepository.



After Creating Product Repository Class. For Using It 

Ok we have just created our ProductRepository class.
Made it's constructor and now are going to ensure other files can also use it .

For example : 
we had 
    public class ProductRepository{
        .....
    }
Lets say our Controller wants to use the methods inside it... For example: GetAllAsync() method
We want to use this method.
Therefore, we will create object of ProductRepository to call it.

private readonly ProductRepository _productRepository;

But , the problem is some1 using This particular repository must also include the different kinds of dependencies the ProductRepository has .
Some1 is using the methods of the ProductRepository.
So, To use a method we need to have the dependencies of it installed too :?

Sounds a-bit hassle right?
So ASP.net provides us with a important Concept called Dependency Injection.
Basically, in this part what we do is :
1. We register the repository with Asp.Net Dependency Injection Container in program.cs .

Note: This wont immediately create the repository object.
[but the action we want is  similar to the repository object]

2. So we did builder.Services.AddScoped<ProductRepository>();
What this AddScoped does is it tells our program.cs/builder that : When something needs a ProductRepository: you will need to provide instance
according to the Scoped lifetime.
